/* ============================================================
   SBL Geography Tutor — Option E: Leisure, Tourism and Sport
   (Paper 1, Optional Themes) lesson configurations.
   Same reusable tutor engine (sbl-teach-bot.js) as other units.
   Hrefs and titles copied verbatim from sbl-sidebar.js
   "Option E: Leisure, Tourism and Sport" (option-e) navigation
   data.

   IMPORTANT: Leisure, Tourism and Sport is its own optional theme,
   separate from Population/Climate/Resource Consumption (Paper 1
   Core), Food and Health (Option F) and Freshwater (Option A).
   This file defines its OWN separate lesson order,
   SBL_LT_LESSON_ORDER, so Spaced Retrieval for this theme is a
   distinct pool and does NOT get concatenated onto any other
   unit's lesson order. Pages in this theme should only include
   this lesson file.

   NOTE: at the teacher's request, the "checkpoint quiz" lesson
   originally listed as Topic 1 Lesson 4 in the sidebar
   ("Changing leisure patterns checkpoint quiz",
   t1-changing/l4-changing) was removed from the sidebar's topic
   list entirely (2026-08-18) and is deliberately NOT built here or
   referenced in SBL_LT_LESSON_ORDER. The teacher's material
   originally intended for that slot (tourism hotspots, primary and
   secondary touristic resources, Butler's Model) was instead used
   to build LT04, which is the exact content match for the
   sidebar's Topic 2 Lesson 1 ("What are the factors influencing
   tourism hotspots?"). The equivalent "Food and Health Checkpoint"
   entry (Option F, t1-measuring-food/l5-food-and-health) was
   likewise removed from the sidebar at the same time; it was never
   built into sbl-fh-lessons.js.

   Lesson IDs: LT01-LT17, short numeric, matching the sidebar's
   4-topic structure (Topic 1: Changing leisure patterns, 3
   lessons after the checkpoint quiz was removed; Topic 2: Tourism
   and sport at a local and national scale, 5 lessons; Topic 3:
   Tourism and sport at the international scale, 5 lessons; Topic
   4: Managing tourism and sport for the future, 4 lessons).

   BUILD STATUS: Topic 1 (LT01-LT03) and Topic 2 (LT04-LT08)
   complete. LT01's definitions, OECD hours-worked graph, reasons-
   for-growth table and OECD time-use/gender-gap charts were
   provided by the teacher; the categorisation of touristic
   activities (by cost/duration/destination) and sporting
   activities (by cost/popularity/site) was built from established
   syllabus knowledge since no specific source material was given
   for this part. LT02 uses the teacher's income-leisure trade-off
   graph and gender-gap chart; its required "USA and China"
   contrasting case study was independently researched and verified
   via web search (China's rapidly growing domestic/outbound
   tourism vs the USA's comparatively low, "vacation deprived"
   leisure-time uptake despite high income), since the teacher's
   material did not include this case study's specific facts — flag
   this to the teacher if a different pair of contrasting examples
   is preferred. LT03 uses the teacher's factors list, the
   InThinking "Cost is the biggest barrier" article, the place-of-
   residence/passport-power material and the EU gender-equality-in-
   sport document; Plog's model of tourist personality types was
   verified via web search since the teacher's slide referenced it
   by name without providing the stage descriptions. LT04 uses the
   teacher's key-terms table and tourism-hotspots worksheet answers
   (verbatim), the honeypot diamond-9 ranking factors, and Butler's
   Model of Tourism Area Life Cycle, which was built from
   established/verified knowledge since the teacher's material
   referenced it by name (in the success criteria) without
   providing the stage-by-stage content. LT05 uses the teacher's
   key-concepts box (sphere of influence, population threshold,
   leisure hierarchy, verbatim) and intra-urban spatial pattern
   diagram; the definition of "range" was not supplied and was
   built from established geography knowledge. LT06 (Premier
   League/Nottingham Forest case study) was built almost entirely
   from independently verified web research — the teacher's
   material was prompts/questions rather than source content — flag
   this to the teacher to confirm the level of case-study detail is
   appropriate. LT07 uses the teacher's costs/benefits table
   (Olympics/World Cup examples, verbatim) plus an independently
   researched Full Moon Party (Koh Phangan, Thailand) case study,
   since the teacher's material only linked a video and asked
   students to research it themselves. LT08 (Glastonbury) uses the
   teacher's map-skills worksheet, advantages/disadvantages
   template and scale statistics; the specific OS grid references,
   road names and nearby villages were independently verified via
   web search (Worthy Farm's precise six-figure grid reference and
   the exact feature/elevation at grid reference 595400 could NOT
   be confirmed from public sources and are flagged as
   unconfirmed rather than guessed). IMPORTANT: the teacher's
   statistics (900 shops, 5,000 toilets, 3,000,000 gallons of
   water, 1,000,000+ sqm camping) were labelled "2020," but
   Glastonbury Festival was actually CANCELLED in 2020 and 2021 due
   to COVID-19 — web research strongly suggests these figures are
   from the 2017 festival (its 35th anniversary) instead. LT08 uses
   the statistics but relabels them as "a recent pre-pandemic
   edition" rather than repeating the incorrect 2020 date — flag
   this correction to the teacher.
   Topics 3 and 4 (LT09-LT17) still pending.
   ============================================================ */
